In LaTeX, if you want to align the caption of a table to the left without using the `caption` package, you can manually adjust the alignment of the caption text. By default, captions are centered. However, you can use the `\makebox` or `\raggedright` commands within the `\caption` to achieve left alignment.

Here's a basic way to do it using the `\makebox` command:

```latex
\documentclass{article}

\begin{document}

\begin{table}[h]
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{|c|c|}
    \hline
    Column 1 & Column 2 \\
    \hline
    Data 1 & Data 2 \\
    Data 3 & Data 4 \\
    \hline
  \end{tabular}
  \caption{\makebox[0pt][l]{This is a left-aligned caption}}
\end{table}

\end{document}
```

Explanation:
- `\makebox[0pt][l]{...}` creates a zero-width box with left-aligned content inside the `\caption`. The first argument is the width, set to `0pt` to make it effectively zero, thus forcing left alignment within the existing space constraints.

Alternatively, you can use `\raggedright` in combination with `\parbox` within the caption:

```latex
\documentclass{article}

\begin{document}

\begin{table}[h]
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{|c|c|}
    \hline
    Column 1 & Column 2 \\
    \hline
    Data 1 & Data 2 \\
    Data 3 & Data 4 \\
    \hline
  \end{tabular}
  \caption{\parbox[t]{\textwidth}{\raggedright This is a left-aligned caption}}
\end{table}

\end{document}
```

Explanation:
- `\parbox[t]{\textwidth}{\raggedright ...}` wraps the caption text in a `\parbox` that spans the entire width of the text and applies `\raggedright` to align it left.

Both methods allow for customizing the caption text alignment without relying on external packages.

